1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:05,530
The brush clears the stone gate of a fort, with a wall of heavy timber beside it.

2
00:00:05,530 --> 00:00:13,857
Greek visitors described Pataliputra as a great city walled in wood. Archaeologists later found the wooden posts in the soil.

3
00:00:13,857 --> 00:00:16,811
What did a wooden city leave behind?

4
00:00:33,117 --> 00:00:38,443
How do we know? Archaeologists dug up the Mauryan palace site at Kumrahar.

5
00:00:38,443 --> 00:00:42,782
The Arthashastra, a book on statecraft, is linked with Kautilya.

6
00:00:42,782 --> 00:00:49,967
Megasthenes, a Greek envoy, wrote an account called Indika, now known only in quotations.

7
00:01:04,750 --> 00:01:12,727
The Maurya empire was founded by Chandragupta Maurya. By tradition he was helped by Kautilya, also called Chanakya.

8
00:01:12,727 --> 00:01:18,681
About 321 BCE, they overthrew the Nandas, and took Pataliputra.

9
00:01:18,681 --> 00:01:23,924
Chandragupta then turned to the north-west, which Alexander had left in disorder.

10
00:01:23,924 --> 00:01:31,119
About 305 BCE, he faced Seleucus Nicator, the Greek ruler in the east. They made peace.

11
00:01:31,119 --> 00:01:39,779
Seleucus gave up lands in the west, in Afghanistan and Baluchistan. In return, Chandragupta gave him five hundred elephants.

12
00:01:39,779 --> 00:01:43,881
Seleucus sent an envoy, Megasthenes, to Pataliputra.

13
00:01:43,881 --> 00:01:49,661
He wrote that the city was guarded by a wooden wall with five hundred and seventy towers and sixty-four gates.

14
00:01:49,661 --> 00:01:59,113
Chandragupta ruled until about 297 BCE. Jain tradition says that he gave up his throne, and went south to Shravanabelagola.

15
00:01:59,113 --> 00:02:06,280
His son Bindusara carried on the work. By then the empire stretched from the Himalayas deep into the south.

16
00:02:21,083 --> 00:02:26,696
Old name, new name. Greek writers called Chandragupta Sandrocottus.

17
00:02:26,696 --> 00:02:32,919
In 1793, Sir William Jones noticed that the Greek name matched Chandragupta.

18
00:02:32,919 --> 00:02:36,878
That link helped to fix the dates of early Indian history.

19
00:02:51,683 --> 00:03:01,220
Carve this tip into memory. Nanda fall, Seleucus peace. Megasthenes, wooden walls. Four facts in the order they happened.

20
00:03:01,220 --> 00:03:04,588
They carry the entire reign of Chandragupta.

21
00:03:19,383 --> 00:03:35,375
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Name the capital of the Mauryan empire. The answer: Pataliputra. Number two.

22
00:03:35,375 --> 00:03:47,093
Which Greek ruler did Chandragupta make peace with? The answer: Seleucus Nicator. Number three.

23
00:03:47,093 --> 00:03:59,129
Name the Greek envoy who wrote Indika. The answer: Megasthenes. Now one line for Mains.

24
00:03:59,129 --> 00:04:03,320
Evaluate the sources for the study of the Mauryan empire.

25
00:04:22,117 --> 00:04:29,791
The fort goes into its case. Pataliputra was walled in wood and ruled an empire. Three facts to take away.

26
00:04:29,791 --> 00:04:39,093
A gate of Pataliputra, the capital. Chandragupta ruled until about 297 BCE. Megasthenes wrote Indika.

27
00:04:39,093 --> 00:04:43,045
Next gallery: Inside the Mauryan State.

